WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.996
Two floors of an office look exactly alike.

00:00:02.996 --> 00:00:06.428
Why does a perfectly good mapping system fold them into one?

00:00:09.332 --> 00:00:10.319
Hi, I'm Ondine!

00:00:10.319 --> 00:00:14.268
Today, an open system that keeps a building's floors apart, and two

00:00:14.268 --> 00:00:18.216
more tools with the same habit: refusing an answer they cannot support.

00:00:20.092 --> 00:00:20.904
Chapter one.

00:00:20.904 --> 00:00:22.528
Why identical floors merge.

00:00:26.508 --> 00:00:29.239
A robot walks the corridor on floor one.

00:00:29.239 --> 00:00:32.994
Three metres below, floor zero has the same corridor, scanned earlier.

00:00:33.414 --> 00:00:38.718
The place recognizer compares descriptors and says, correctly, these two places look the same.

00:00:39.138 --> 00:00:40.414
Registration checks the match.

00:00:40.414 --> 00:00:43.604
The walls really do line up, to a few centimetres.

00:00:44.024 --> 00:00:47.519
So the graph gets a loop factor: precise, consistent, and wrong.

00:00:47.519 --> 00:00:50.378
It says two places three metres apart are one.

00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:54.607
Make it one number: floor one's height.

00:00:54.607 --> 00:00:57.806
The stairs say three metres, give or take fifteen centimetres.

00:00:58.226 --> 00:01:01.438
One false loop says zero, give or take five centimetres.

00:01:01.438 --> 00:01:05.936
It outvotes the stairs nine to one, and floor one drops to thirty centimetres.

00:01:06.356 --> 00:01:10.141
Add the rest of the false loops, and floor one lands on floor zero.

00:01:10.141 --> 00:01:12.033
A barometer would only slow the slide.

00:01:12.453 --> 00:01:15.299
Switch the gate on, and the loops are never written.

00:01:15.299 --> 00:01:18.714
Floor one goes back to three metres, however many matches there were.

00:01:20.883 --> 00:01:23.076
The project tests this on purpose.

00:01:23.076 --> 00:01:26.001
Two identical floors, joined by stairs, camera only.

00:01:26.421 --> 00:01:30.462
With the floor gate switched off by a debug flag, the recognizer

00:01:30.462 --> 00:01:32.482
proposes thirty five matches across floors.

00:01:32.902 --> 00:01:35.624
All of them are accepted, along with six real ones.

00:01:36.044 --> 00:01:38.955
Thirty five of forty one loops join different floors.

00:01:38.955 --> 00:01:42.837
The map ends with one floor instead of two, and the test

00:01:42.837 --> 00:01:44.455
passes only because it collapsed.

00:01:46.342 --> 00:01:46.943
Chapter two.

00:01:46.943 --> 00:01:48.145
A graph per floor.

00:01:52.558 --> 00:01:53.918
The idea is blunt.

00:01:53.918 --> 00:01:57.998
Keep a separate map for every floor, refuse every loop closure between

00:01:57.998 --> 00:02:00.379
floors, and join the floors later, offline.

00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:06.521
First, something says the robot left its floor: tracked height, the barometer,

00:02:06.521 --> 00:02:09.080
or the accelerometer feeling an elevator push.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.379
The floor manager waits until everything is calm again.

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:13.744
Then it classifies.

00:02:13.744 --> 00:02:18.156
If the pressure climbed but the camera saw no climb, it was an elevator.

00:02:18.156 --> 00:02:20.677
Otherwise stairs or a ramp, split by slope.

00:02:21.097 --> 00:02:24.157
Finally the climb is snapped to a whole number of storeys, so

00:02:24.157 --> 00:02:26.706
a half landing on the stairs never becomes a floor.

00:02:28.675 --> 00:02:32.261
Every candidate loop passes through a gate before it reaches a graph.

00:02:32.261 --> 00:02:34.652
Open it, and there are three separate checks.

00:02:35.072 --> 00:02:38.468
First, each floor has its own place database, so a scan is

00:02:38.468 --> 00:02:40.449
only ever compared with its own floor.

00:02:40.869 --> 00:02:44.626
Second, a loop whose two ends carry different floor labels is rejected.

00:02:45.046 --> 00:02:49.039
Third, each floor's graph refuses a factor to a node it does not hold.

00:02:49.039 --> 00:02:51.035
No code path can join two floors.

00:02:52.758 --> 00:02:54.773
Their demo shows it in simulation.

00:02:54.773 --> 00:02:58.131
Without the gate, loop closures pile the two floors together.

00:02:58.551 --> 00:03:01.833
With it, each floor stays in its own map, and the stairs join them.

00:03:03.467 --> 00:03:06.708
And on real data, a hand-held LiDAR rides an elevator.

00:03:06.708 --> 00:03:10.597
The accelerometer measures the ride the LiDAR cannot see, the new floor

00:03:10.597 --> 00:03:14.486
gets its own colour, and the walk back down the stairs is

00:03:14.486 --> 00:03:15.783
measured by the odometry.

00:03:17.425 --> 00:03:18.173
Chapter three.

00:03:18.173 --> 00:03:19.669
The same habit elsewhere.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:26.587
Satellite code ranges give a position to a few metres.

00:03:27.007 --> 00:03:31.296
Differencing against a base station and a reference satellite cancels both clocks.

00:03:31.716 --> 00:03:35.593
What remains is a whole number of carrier cycles per satellite, which

00:03:35.593 --> 00:03:37.532
the filter estimates as real numbers.

00:03:37.952 --> 00:03:41.295
An integer least squares search finds the best set and the runner up.

00:03:41.715 --> 00:03:45.808
The library fixes only if the runner up is three times worse.

00:03:45.808 --> 00:03:48.878
Otherwise it stays float, rather than report false confidence.

00:03:50.633 --> 00:03:52.602
A calibration checker does the same.

00:03:52.602 --> 00:03:56.212
Here a LiDAR transform carries an injected yaw error, and the

00:03:56.212 --> 00:03:57.525
points miss the bollards.

00:03:57.945 --> 00:04:02.159
It judges the error against the data's own noise and fails the pair.

00:04:02.159 --> 00:04:06.373
An axis the recording could not observe is marked not measured, never guessed.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:10.420
The floor gate is sound by construction.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:12.041
The rest has thinner evidence.

00:04:12.461 --> 00:04:16.794
The simulator's camera produces feature points, not rendered images, so the visual

00:04:16.794 --> 00:04:20.044
front end's real failures are not in those tests.

00:04:20.464 --> 00:04:25.202
Real multi-floor evidence is one elevator dataset and a few robot bags.

00:04:25.622 --> 00:04:30.277
And matching walls between floors has not yet contributed a constraint on real data.

00:04:31.683 --> 00:04:35.144
The common thread: the safest estimator is one that cannot write

00:04:35.144 --> 00:04:36.402
the wrong answer down.

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:38.907
To recap.

00:04:38.907 --> 00:04:41.934
One map per floor, with no loops between.

00:04:41.934 --> 00:04:44.583
Integers fixed only past a ratio test.

00:04:44.583 --> 00:04:46.853
And calibration checked, axis by axis.

00:04:47.273 --> 00:04:49.406
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:49.406 --> 00:04:50.016
I'm Ondine.

00:04:50.016 --> 00:04:50.320
Bye!
